1,0,N$///For the convenience in identifying following morphospecies have been added to the key, on account of the evidence in Noordeloos (2004): E. austriacum, E. cedretorum, E. insidiosum, E. juniperinum, E. klofacianum, E. lidbergii and E. wynnei. ///Also E. violaceozonatum Noordel. & Liiv is included, despite its placement in the Inocephalus-Cyanula clade.

2,3 $ Lamellae coloured when young
2,7 $ Lamellae white when young 

3,0 $ Lamellae firstly brownish then reddish brown with fimbriate concolorous edge; pileus fibrillose-zonate, lamellae edge entirely sterile with dense clusters of very long (up to 300 µm) subcylindrical cheilocystidia $ E. violaceozonatum 
3,4 $ Lamellae with bluish or violaceous-blue tinge when young, lamellae edge sterile or heterogeneous, cheilocystidia of another type, smaller

4,0 $ Lamellae violaceous-blue with brownish violaceous edge when young; fruitbodies entirely deep violaceous-blue; on wood, preferably of Alnus///n° 2 $ E. euchroum 
4,5 $ Lamellae pale to moderately dark blue or violaceous-blue with concolorous or slightly paler edge when young

5,0 $ Pileus and stipe with the same deep blue colour, lamellae bluish with concolorous or slightly paler edge; spores 7.5–11.5 × 6.0–8.0 µm///n° 13. $ E. lepidissimum
5,6 $ Pileus pinkish lilaceous to violaceous, stipe dark blue

6,0 $ Spores with blunt angles, cheilocystidia absent///n° 10a $ E. callichroum var. callichroum
6,0 $ Spores with moderately pronounced angles, cheilocystidia broadly clavate, intermixed with basidia///n° 10b $ E. callichroum var. venustum 

7,8 $ Pileus and stipe blue or violaceous-blue
7,14 $ Pileus and stipe violaceous, brown-violaceous or pinkish lilaceous

8,0 $ Spores isodiametrical, up to 9 µm diam . $ E. klofacianum
8,9 $ Spores heterodiametrical

9,10 $ Spores 6.5–8.5(–9.0) × 5.5–6.5 µm, pileus conical to campanulate, hardly expanding, stipe smooth to subfibrillose
9,11 $ Spores larger, pileus hemisphaerical, convex to applanate, stipe fibrillose or minutely squamulose

10,0 $ Spores moderately angled, stipe smooth to subfibrillos $ E. coelestinum
10,0 $ Spores weakly angled, stipe fibrillose or minutely squamulose///n° 12 $ E. percoelestinum

11,0 $ Spores distinctly nodulose, 8.5–11.5 × 6.0–7.0 µm, pileus convex to applanate///n° 1 $ E. chytrophilum 
11,12 $ Spores with 5–7 pronounced angles

12,0 $ Cheilocystidia absent .$ E. cedretorum
12,13 $ Cheilocystidia present

13,0 $ Fruitbody collybioid///n° 7 $ E. dichroum
13,0 $ Fruitbody tricholomatoid///n° 8 $ E. eugenei

14,0 $ Spores with 7–9 weak angles; 9.0–12.2 × 6.0–8.2 µm; pileus a tender pinkish ochre with brown with lilac or violaceous tint at centre $ E. austriacum
14,15 $ Spores with 5–7 pronounced angles

15,0 $ Fruitbodies very small; pileus up to 10 mm, pink $ E. lidbergii
15,0 $ Fruitbodies larger; pileus 20–40 mm, greyish brown with lilaceous tinge. $ E. allochroum

16,0 $ Spores 10.0–16.0(–18.0) × 7.0–10.0 µm; pileus warm brown contrasting with the blue stipe $ E. wynnei 
16,17 $ Spores < 10.0–16.0(–18.0) × 7.0–10.0 µm

17,18 $ Spores with 5–7 pronounced angles
17,19 $ Spores nodulose or with 6–9 weak angles

18,0 $ Stipe blue, flocculose-squamulose///n° 7 $ E. dichroum (pale form)
18,0 $ Stipe violaceous, longitudinally fibrillose///n° 9 $ E. allochroum

19,0 $ Spores distinctly nodulose///n° 5 $ E. sublaevisporum
19,20 $ Spores with 6–9 weak angles

20,21 $ Stipe glabrous, polished
20,23 $ Stipe longitudinally fibrillose or squamulose

21,22 $ Spores isodiametrical to subisodiametrical
21,0 $ Spores heterodiametrical $ E. insidiosum

22,0 $ Spores 8.0–10.0 × 6.0–8.0 µm; pileus dark brown when young $ E. juniperinum
22,0 $ Spores 7.5–9.0 × 6.5–8.5 µm; pileus blue when young $ E. klofacianum

23,24 $ Stipe flocculose-squamulose
23,26 $ Stipe fibrillose-striate

24,0 $ Pileus pale ochraceous; on wood of Alnus, early in season ///n° 6b $ E. tjallingiorum var. alnetorum
24,25 $ Pileus brown or grey-brown

25,0 $ Lamellae edge heterogeneous, cheilocystidia lageniform, intermixed with basidia; on soil or on wood of deciduous trees///n° 6a $ E. tjallingiorum var. tjallingiorum
25,0 $ Lamellae edge sterile, cheilocystidia as strands of terminal elements of hyphae of trama hymenial///n° 6c $ E. tjallingiorum var. laricinum

26,0 $ Pileipellis with intracellular pigment; cheilocystidia absent; smell farinaceous; on wood of deciduous trees, mainly of Fagus///n° 4 $ E. placidum 
26,0 $ Pileipellis with both intracellular and encrusting pigment; cheilocystidia present or absent; smell none; on soil or on wood of coniferous trees///n° 3 $ E. lampropus
